program TriangleArea;
var
  base, height: real;
  area: real;
begin
  writeln('Enter the base of the triangle: ');
  readln(base);
  writeln('Enter the height of the triangle: ');
  readln(height);
  area := 0.5 * base * height;
  writeln('The area of the triangle is: ', area:0:2);
  readln;
end. 

In this program, we declare three variables: base (represents the base of the triangle), height (represents the height of the triangle), and area (represents the calculated area of the triangle).

We then prompt the user to enter the base and height values by using the "writeln" and "readln" functions.

After that, we calculate the area of the triangle by using the formula "0.5 * base * height". We use the assignment operator (":=") to set the value of the "area" variable.

Finally, we display the calculated area using the "writeln" function, formatting the output to show two decimal places.

When we run the program, it will display the message to enter the base and height values. After the user inputs the values, the program calculates the area of the triangle and displays the result.

